rner</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 SF are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to SEA \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Jadarian Price</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>SEA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>SEA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jadarian Price</strong> (12 carries, 52 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though SEA's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 82 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>AJ Barner</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 26 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>SF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>SF are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 SF project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 73 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Mike Evans</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, SF funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 60 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>George Kittle</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 63 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"SF","col":"#E8506A","players":[{"name":"Brock Purdy","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":264.77,"book":null,"over_th":248.13,"under_th":275.77,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.79,"book":null,"over_th":1.06,"under_th":1.45,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":22.8,"fair":"7/2","book":null,"value":null}},{"name":"Christian McCaffrey","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":72.65,"book":null,"over_th":57.16,"under_th":70.77,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},